WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.921
The Carnatic departed at 6:30 p.m. on November 7 and steamed toward Yokohama, Japan.

2
00:00:05.922 --> 00:00:13.700
The ship was completely booked, and only two staterooms in the rear were unoccupied, those reserved for Mr. Fogg and Aouda.

3
00:00:13.701 --> 00:00:20.971
Shortly after Fix had left Passepartout in the opium den, two waiters lifted him to the bed reserved for smokers.

4
00:00:20.972 --> 00:00:26.114
Three hours later he woke up and fought off the hypnotic influence of the drug.

5
00:00:26.115 --> 00:00:31.441
He hurried to the steamer and, when he reached his room onboard, immediately passed out.

6
00:00:31.442 --> 00:00:37.849
When Passepartout woke up the next morning, he staggered onto the deck, wondering what Mr. Fogg would say.

7
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:45.292
"At least I didn't miss the steamer, which is the most important thing!" he said to himself, as he sipped some strong black coffee.

8
00:00:45.293 --> 00:00:49.759
Passepartout suddenly thought of Fix and how the man had left him in the tavern.

9
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.940
"As for that rascal, I hope we are rid of him," he said. "A

10
00:00:53.941 --> 00:01:03.583
detective on the track of Mr. Fogg, accusing my master of robbing the Bank of England—ridiculous! Mr. Fogg is not a robber." Passepartout

11
00:01:03.584 --> 00:01:08.311
finished his breakfast and then descended to the staterooms. Mr.

12
00:01:08.312 --> 00:01:14.014
Fogg and Aouda were nowhere to be found! There was no luggage . . . nothing. Passepartout

13
00:01:14.015 --> 00:01:17.256
wondered if he was even on the right ship. He

14
00:01:17.257 --> 00:01:21.197
rushed to the purser and asked, "Am I on the Carnatic ?"

15
00:01:21.198 --> 00:01:25.501
"Yes, on the way to Yokohama," replied the purser, a little surprised.

16
00:01:25.502 --> 00:01:28.948
"May I look at the passenger list?" asked Passepartout.

17
00:01:28.949 --> 00:01:33.873
"Of course." The purser had the list with him and showed Passepartout.

18
00:01:33.874 --> 00:01:38.364
When he had read every name, Passepartout fell thunderstruck on a seat.

19
00:01:38.365 --> 00:01:44.155
Mr. Fogg and Aouda were not on the passenger list! Now he realized what had happened.

20
00:01:44.156 --> 00:01:48.781
The time of departure had changed, and he had been unable to tell Mr. Fogg.

21
00:01:48.782 --> 00:01:51.695
Mr. Fogg and Aouda had missed the steamer!

22
00:01:51.696 --> 00:01:54.796
Passepartout felt sick to his stomach with rage.

23
00:01:54.797 --> 00:02:01.532
He tore at his hair and vowed that, if he ever saw Fix again, he would even the score with him indeed!

24
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:05.905
After his anger calmed, Passepartout thought through his situation.

25
00:02:05.906 --> 00:02:08.626
It was certainly not an attractive one.

26
00:02:08.627 --> 00:02:11.222
He was on the way to Japan with no money.

27
00:02:11.223 --> 00:02:13.820
And what was he going to do when he got there?

28
00:02:13.821 --> 00:02:18.588
At dawn on November 13, the Carnatic reached the port of Yokohama.

29
00:02:18.589 --> 00:02:24.144
Passepartout went ashore. He had nothing better to do than wander aimlessly through the city.

30
00:02:24.145 --> 00:02:31.296
In the crowded streets, he saw all sorts of people: priests in white robes, police officers with black pointed hats,

31
00:02:31.297 --> 00:02:37.824
sailors in blue cotton, and royal guards in stiff armor with long thin swords on their backs.

32
00:02:37.825 --> 00:02:46.833
He also saw beautiful groves, tall sacred gates, bridges hidden in the midst of bamboo, and temples shaded by immense cedar trees. By

33
00:02:46.834 --> 00:02:50.840
the next morning, hunger gnawed at Passepartout's empty stomach. With

34
00:02:50.841 --> 00:02:58.276
no money, he thought of selling his precious family watch but changed his mind. He would rather starve first! He

35
00:02:58.277 --> 00:03:05.081
decided he could earn some money as a vagabond performer but then thought he was too well dressed to be believable. After

36
00:03:05.082 --> 00:03:09.312
a long search, he found a small shop that bought old clothes. He

37
00:03:09.313 --> 00:03:16.285
asked the owner if he could exchange his European clothes for an old Japanese coat and a faded turban he saw in the window. The

38
00:03:16.286 --> 00:03:20.031
owner also gave him a few pieces of silver in the trade. Passepartout

39
00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:26.090
immediately found a food stand and ate what little breakfast he could afford. "Now,"

40
00:03:26.091 --> 00:03:32.174
he thought to himself, "I mustn't lose my head. I must consider how I am going to feed myself." He

41
00:03:32.175 --> 00:03:39.114
walked toward the docks, having decided that passengers from visiting steamers would be the most generous customers. On

42
00:03:39.115 --> 00:03:42.618
the way he was surprised to see a sign in English. It

43
00:03:42.619 --> 00:03:43.485
read: Japanese

44
00:03:43.486 --> 00:03:46.718
Acrobats The Long Noses! Come

45
00:03:46.719 --> 00:03:52.184
and see them before their departure to the United States! Grand Attraction! Passepartout

46
00:03:52.185 --> 00:03:56.081
was excited. He had been an acrobat in his youth. If

47
00:03:56.082 --> 00:04:02.173
he was hired, he could find himself on the way to the United States, exactly where Mr. Fogg was headed! A

48
00:04:02.174 --> 00:04:06.984
quarter of an hour later, Passepartout stopped before a large, colorful tent. He

49
00:04:06.985 --> 00:04:10.446
entered and asked for the owner of the show. Mr.

50
00:04:10.447 --> 00:04:14.759
Batulcar appeared immediately and interviewed Passepartout. "You

51
00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.298
are pretty strong, eh?" asked Mr. Batulcar. "Especially

52
00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:22.070
after a good meal!" responded Passepartout. "And

53
00:04:22.071 --> 00:04:25.438
can you sing?" Mr. Batulcar asked. "Yes,"

54
00:04:25.439 --> 00:04:28.270
said Passepartout. "But

55
00:04:28.271 --> 00:04:37.066
can you sing standing on your head, with a top spinning on your left foot and a sword balanced on your right?" asked Mr. Batulcar. "I

56
00:04:37.067 --> 00:04:41.825
think so," said Passepartout, and he proceeded to demonstrate his skill. "You are

57
00:04:41.826 --> 00:04:48.302
hired!" exclaimed Mr. Batulcar. Passepartout's Guidebook

58
00:04:48.303 --> 00:04:55.025
Well, what a day! Wandering the streets of Yokohama with no money and then finding myself hired as an acrobat.

59
00:04:55.026 --> 00:04:59.143
Yokohama certainly is a unique place, where East meets West!

60
00:04:59.144 --> 00:05:07.737
In Japan the Meiji period ("Meiji" means "enlightened rule") began in 1868, and so far it has brought many changes to Japan.

61
00:05:07.738 --> 00:05:13.214
Leaders have decided to model Japan's government, legal system, military, and economy on the West.

62
00:05:13.215 --> 00:05:18.255
And according to a new policy, "knowledge shall be sought throughout the world."

63
00:05:18.256 --> 00:05:23.111
So Japan has employed over three-thousand foreign experts in fields such as science, engineering,

64
00:05:23.112 --> 00:05:26.587
and English language instruction to help modernize society.

65
00:05:26.588 --> 00:05:31.636
The government is also sending many Japanese students overseas to Europe and America to study.

66
00:05:31.637 --> 00:05:34.829
These changes will surely transform the people of Japan.

67
00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:41.581
Perhaps some of them will come to see my acrobat performance!

